You are a principal full-stack engineer and staffing-domain product architect at an enterprise consultancy. You are building the minimum lovable product (MVP) of HireNestOS, the AI-native staffing operating system for HireNest Workforce. Build it from scratch, production-grade, secure by default, with a professional, consistent enterprise UI.

The product must feel like real software that a staffing company runs on (think Greenhouse, Bullhorn or Ceipal), not a demo. Correct data, correct permissions and a clear workflow matter more than visual flourish.

Before you write code, restate the scope in five bullets and list the screens you will build. Then build everything in one pass. Do not stop at a skeleton.

1. NON-NEGOTIABLE RULES (read twice)
Supabase is the only backend and the only source of truth. The database, RLS policies, functions and storage bucket ALREADY EXIST. Do not create, alter or migrate tables. Do not write SQL. Do not invent tables, columns, enums or RPC names. Use exactly the contract in Section 5. If something seems missing, show a clear "Not available in MVP" state. Do not fake it.
No mock, sample, hardcoded or random data anywhere. Every list, count, score and chart comes from Supabase. Empty database means empty states with a helpful call to action.
No paid AI. Do NOT use Gemini. Do not import @google/genai or any Google AI SDK. Do not call any LLM API. The owner's policy is Ollama-only, no paid fallback. Matching in the MVP is done in the database by a deterministic engine (candidate_matches). AI resume parsing and AI screening interviews are Phase 2. Show them as clearly labelled "Coming soon" panels behind the platform_settings.ai_screening_enabled flag, which is false by default.
Security is enforced by the database. The UI is never the security boundary. Still, the UI must:
only use the anon/publishable key (VITE_SUPABASE_URL, VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY); never a service-role key, and never hardcode keys;
never trust user_metadata for role; read the role from public.profiles;
never log PII (email, phone, resume text) to the console, analytics or error messages;
route by role and hide actions a role cannot perform, but always handle 42501 / "FORBIDDEN" errors gracefully.
Pipeline writes go through RPCs only. Never insert or update candidates creation, submissions, interviews, requirement_vendor_shares, match overrides or requirement status directly. Use the RPCs in Section 5.4. Direct table writes are allowed only where Section 5.3 says so.
A potential match is NOT an application. Only apply_to_requirement (direct candidate) or submit_candidate (vendor/HQ) creates a pipeline record.
Source attribution must always be correct and visible: vendor (shows vendor agency name), direct (never show a vendor), internal (HireNest sourced). The candidate's original source, current vendor association and submitter for a specific requirement are three different things. Display them separately.
Recruiter override wins. Show final_score, and when override_score is set, show "Overridden by recruiter" with the reason.
Bounded queries only. Every list uses server-side pagination (.range(from, to) with count: 'exact'), default page size 25, and filters applied in the query, not in the browser.
Human decisions are final. Scores are advisory. Every consequential action (reject, withdraw, share with client, offer, override, approve) needs a confirmation dialog, and a reason where the RPC requires one.
